{
  "dom_id": "step-e2969",
  "name": "Example: Identifying Shapes Similar to a Given Polygon",
  "problem": "Which of the following quadrilaterals are similar to $\\mathcal{R}$ (other than $\\mathcal R$ itself)? \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14199831/assets/5a7c9208a16a639ef6b08855a6caaacb39c121dd4ccf45271d98fcc70f1a8fed.png)",
  "worked_solution": "First, notice that $\\mathcal{R}$ is a rectangle, meaning all the angles measure $90^\\circ,$ and it has $2$ pairs of parallel congruent sides.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given polygons.\n\n- $\\mathcal{Q}$ is *not* similar to $\\mathcal{R}.$ The polygon $\\mathcal{Q}$ is a rectangle, and the corresponding angles are congruent, but the ratios of the lengths of the corresponding sides are not the same: \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{6}\\neq\\dfrac{3}{3}\n$$\n\n- $\\mathcal{P}$ is similar to $\\mathcal{R}.$ Indeed, $\\mathcal{P}$ is a rectangle. Furthermore, the corresponding angles are congruent, and the ratios of the lengths of the corresponding sides are the same:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{6}=\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\n- $\\mathcal{S}$ is *not* similar to $\\mathcal{R}$ because $\\mathcal{S}$ is not a rectangle (it's a parallelogram).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\mathcal{P}$ only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
